/target/
**/target/
/.idea/
*.lock

# Temporary diagnostic outputs (generated during local builds / tests)
/build-log.txt
/test-output.txt
/test-full-output.txt

# Stray `rustc --out-dir .` artifacts (occur when a quick rustc invocation
# runs in the workspace root instead of going through `cargo`).
/rust_out.exe
/rust_out.pdb

# Agent worktree scratch (created by subagents with isolation: "worktree").
/.claude/worktrees/
# graphify knowledge-graph artifacts (rebuilt per-machine by the post-commit hook)
graphify-out/

# mdBook build output (the source is book/src/; CI rebuilds for GitHub Pages)
/book/output/

# Owner-provided PBR material packs (large, externally-sourced texture sets — never
# committed; see assets/materials/README.md for the folder convention).
/assets/materials/*
!/assets/materials/README.md

# VG-R0 density-census corpus (plan section 4.3): the MANIFEST is tracked, the payload is not.
# Mirrors the assets/materials rule above. A high-poly corpus is not small by any definition that
# keeps this repo cloneable, there is no .gitattributes (so no Git LFS seam), and git history is
# immutable -- a corpus committed once is carried by every clone forever. Pinned instead by the
# sha256 columns in CORPUS.toml, which scripts/fetch_corpus.ps1 verifies before extracting.
/assets/vg_corpus/*
!/assets/vg_corpus/CORPUS.toml
!/assets/vg_corpus/README.md

# Pristine pre-conversion normal maps left behind by scripts/normal_ogl_to_dx.py.
# The green-channel inversion is its own inverse, so the committed DirectX map fully
# determines the OpenGL source — these backups are a local convenience, not history.
*.ogl.bak

# Local drop folder for third-party reference material (papers, vendor sample code).
# Externally copyrighted and large — cite these from docs/ instead of vendoring them.
/info/

# The decidability floor's SESSION FILE, written by `vg_decidability_floor_measure` and read by
# `Floor::from_session_file` -- deliberately NOT committed, and the reason is the instrument's own
# rule rather than tidiness. `docs/VG-DECIDABILITY-FLOOR.md` states it twice: "a floor established
# on a different instrument bounds nothing about this one", and "a floor measured yesterday does
# not bound a delta measured today". A committed floor would let a fresh clone on another box
# resolve verdicts against a number that bounds nothing there. With the file absent, `resolve`
# returns `NotResolved` until someone re-measures on the machine making the claim -- which is the
# honest answer, and it is what rung 7b's protocol exists to produce.
#
# It was already untracked before this line existed; the line is here so that "absent" is a
# DECISION with a reason rather than an omission nobody noticed.
/docs/PROFILING-FLOOR.toml
